		{
			"id": "109882152014613352",
			"parentId": "109882052840463912",
			"url": "https://ruby.social/@redknitin/109882151942247759",
			"author": {
				"name": "Nitin",
				"url": "https://ruby.social/@redknitin",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/631/753/547/550/276/original/ae5ee582b7596d8d.png"
			},
			"date": "2023-02-17T21:16:18.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://techhub.social/@develwithoutacause\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>develwithoutacause</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> A couple of years ago, the default on installing software was to have no authentication &amp; to listen for network connections to make it easy for someone to try it. Turns out they didn’t change the defaults when going into a production environment &amp; then they complained about getting hacked. Media firms would write in headlines, “Software X was hacked,” as though trying to say burglars hacked a door when instead the door was wide open.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "109882741860007534",
